Continue developing the existing Shock Calibration Studio.

Your immediate objective is to deliver a working, interactive
AI-powered quantitative analytics application.

The historical data ingestion, validation, Parquet/DuckDB
storage, quantitative engine, and backend APIs have already
been implemented. Build on these components.

WORKING APPROACH

Focus on execution and visible functional progress.

This is a POC, so keep engineering practical.
Avoid unnecessary production-grade infrastructure,
excessive abstractions, and speculative requirements.

Make reasonable technical decisions independently.
Implement, test, fix issues, and continue progressing.
Do not repeatedly stop for assessments or approvals.

1. BUILD THE CONVERSATIONAL ANALYTICS WORKSPACE

Create a modern financial analytics interface inspired
by ChatGPT and professional quantitative research tools.

Left sidebar:
- Conversation history
- Searchable risk-factor catalogue
- Available historical datasets
- Historical data ingestion

Center:
- Full conversational chatbot
- Natural-language financial questions
- Contextual follow-up questions
- Clear quantitative explanations
- Suggested analytical questions

Right panel:
- Interactive historical time-series charts
- Rolling market movements
- Historical shock distributions
- Percentiles and statistical summaries
- Comparison of multiple risk factors
- Historical coverage and calculation methodology

Make the interface polished, intuitive, and responsive.

2. CONNECT THE CHATBOT TO THE QUANTITATIVE ENGINE

Implement the complete workflow:

Natural-language question
→ Financial intent interpretation
→ Market factor identification
→ Historical data retrieval from DuckDB
→ Python quantitative calculations
→ Analytical response
→ Interactive visualizations

Support flexible financial questions rather than
a fixed set of predefined queries.

Reuse existing calculation tools and APIs.

Ensure financial movement conventions are appropriate
to the selected instrument.

3. ENABLE REAL LLM CAPABILITIES

Integrate an available approved enterprise LLM
using the existing environment and integrations.

Enable tool calling, financial reasoning, factor
discovery, and conversational context.

Python should execute numerical calculations.
The LLM should interpret questions, select tools,
and explain the computed results.

If a real LLM connection is unavailable, continue
building the complete application using the existing
calculation interface.

Keep the integration ready for an approved model.
Clearly distinguish local query handling from
genuine LLM-powered interaction.

4. CONTINUE HISTORICAL DATA INGESTION

Expand beyond the existing FX dataset.

Support interest rates, FX, credit, equity,
and commodity risk factors.

Use Olympus HDS through authorized access.

Adapt extraction to Olympus limitations:
- Query smaller historical periods where useful.
- Persist successful extraction batches.
- Resume from previously completed batches.
- Respect enforced query quotas.
- Continue application development independently
  when Olympus is unavailable.

Use existing CSV/Parquet imports whenever historical
data is already available.

Maintain appropriate data-quality checks and
financial instrument identification.

Do not make complete historical coverage a
prerequisite for application development.

5. IMPLEMENT AND RUN

Develop the frontend and backend together.

Connect the conversational interface to real
historical calculations.

Make charts and analytical panels respond dynamically
to the user's questions.

Run the application, resolve integration errors,
and verify the complete user interaction.

Continue improving functionality as you work.

EXPECTED RESULT

A running Shock Calibration Studio where I can:

- Ask natural-language quantitative questions.
- Explore available financial market factors.
- Calculate historical shocks and percentiles.
- Visualize historical market movements.
- Compare financial instruments.
- Import and analyze additional historical datasets.
- Ask follow-up questions within the conversation.

Prioritize delivering a usable application now.

At completion, provide the application URL, a brief
summary of implemented functionality, and any remaining
external dependencies.

Proceed with implementation.
